  <h1>Types of Defamation Claims</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Libel (written defamation)</h2><p>Written or recorded false statements: newspaper articles, online posts, social media, emails, reviews, and broadcast content. Higher baseline damages than slander because publication is permanent and can spread widely.</p>
  <h2>Slander (spoken defamation)</h2><p>Oral false statements. Generally requires proof of actual damages (special damages) unless the statement is slander per se. Harder to prove because of lack of a written record.</p>
  <h2>Defamation per se</h2><p>Categories where damages are presumed: criminal conduct, loathsome disease, professional unfitness, and sexual misconduct. No need to prove specific economic harm &mdash; the jury may award general damages based on presumed reputational harm.</p>
  <h2>Trade libel / commercial disparagement</h2><p>False statements about a business&rsquo;s products or services that cause economic harm. The false statement must be about the product or business (not the owner personally). Requires proof of actual economic loss.</p>
  <h2>False light</h2><p>A related privacy tort: placing someone in a false light before the public in a way that a reasonable person would find highly objectionable. Requires the same actual malice standard as defamation for public figures in most states.</p>
